FILE: app/src/turntable.ts
```ts
import { interpolateArcRotateCamera } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import type { ArcRotateCamera, SceneContext } from "@babylonjs/lite";

export function spinAround(camera: ArcRotateCamera, scene: SceneContext, turns: number): Promise<void> {
    const targetAlpha = camera.alpha + 2 * Math.PI * turns;
    return interpolateArcRotateCamera(camera, scene, { alpha: targetAlpha });
}
```

I wrote `src/turntable.ts` which delegates to the library's own `interpolateArcRotateCamera` helper, passing only the target `alpha` (start + 2π·turns) so that beta, radius, and target are left untouched. The only thing I was slightly unsure of is whether `interpolateArcRotateCamera` is re-exported from the main `@babylonjs/lite` entry point or lives on a subpath like `@babylonjs/lite/camera/arc-rotate-interpolate`; I assumed the former since your existing imports pull `ArcRotateCamera` and `SceneContext` from the root package, but if it isn't there you'll just need to adjust that one import specifier.
